<section class="reference-section" id="ambarisha-durvasa"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Ambarisha and Durvasa</h2><a href="/ambarisha-durvasa.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 9 · chapters 4–5.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Volume 2. The private source PDF is not distributed by this website.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%202%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Read the supplied second volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press · Sanskrit with Hindi explanation</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>9.4.15–23 · private PDF pp. 31–33</strong><small>Ambarisha regards sovereignty as transient and directs mind, speech, senses and action toward devotion.</small></li><li><strong>9.4.24–35 · PDF pp. 33–35</strong><small>The year-long vow, worship, gifts, the concluding fast and Durvasa’s arrival as an honoured guest.</small></li><li><strong>9.4.36–42 · PDF pp. 35–36</strong><small>Durvasa goes to the Yamuna; the Dvadashi deadline approaches; Ambarisha consults the brahmins and takes water.</small></li><li><strong>9.4.43–50 · PDF pp. 36–37</strong><small>Durvasa’s anger, the fiery creation, Ambarisha’s composure and Sudarshana’s intervention.</small></li><li><strong>9.4.51–59 · PDF pp. 37–39</strong><small>The pursuit across the worlds and Durvasa’s appeals to Brahma and Shiva.</small></li><li><strong>9.4.60–71 · PDF pp. 39–41</strong><small>Durvasa reaches Vishnu; the Lord describes his bond with devotees and directs the sage back to Ambarisha.</small></li><li><strong>9.5.1–13 · PDF pp. 42–44</strong><small>Durvasa returns; Ambarisha prays to Sudarshana for the sage’s protection.</small></li><li><strong>9.5.14–24 · PDF pp. 44–46</strong><small>Durvasa’s praise, the completed hospitality, Ambarisha’s year of waiting and the narrative conclusion.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> The narrative preserves the source’s Vaishnava theology and its conflict between ritual timing and hospitality. It does not present fasting as medical guidance or Ambarisha’s decision as a universal rule. The closing reflection on protection without humiliation is editorial.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated interpretations used once each. The palace, clothing, fiery being, cosmic journey and symbolic still life are imagined. Conflict is depicted without graphic violence.</p></div></section>

